<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Livonians</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Livs</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Livonians, or Livs, are a Finnic people of the coast of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-riga">Gulf of Riga</a> in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latvia">Latvia</a>. In the early 13th century German crusaders conquered and baptised them, and their name was given to the territory of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonia">Livonia</a>. They were gradually absorbed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latvians">Latvians</a>, and the Livonian language survives only as a heritage language.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/livonians.jpg" alt="Livonians" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tasman · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Livonians.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Livonians, or Livs, are one of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finnic-peoples">Finnic peoples</a> indigenous to the Livonian Coast, in northwestern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latvia">Latvia</a> and southwestern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a>, and they spoke Livonian, a Finnic language closely related to Estonian and Finnish. The date of their arrival is disputed. They claim to have lived in their homeland for over 5,000 years, while another view holds that Finnic tribes were pushed into the coastal regions by the Slav migrations of the sixth and seventh centuries CE. They called themselves rāndalist, coast dwellers, and lived mainly by fishing, with some farming. They controlled the trade route of the Daugava River and traded with Gotlanders, Russians and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finns">Finns</a> and later with Germans, Swedes and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danes">Danes</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Missionaries followed the traders. In the 12th century Germans established a base at Uexküll, now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ikskile">Ikšķile</a>. Bishop Meinhard died in 1196, and his successor Berthold of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanover">Hanover</a>, who tried to convert the Livonians by force, was killed in 1198. Albert von Buxhövden then made the Livonian leaders at the mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-dvina">Daugava</a> give him land, and building began in 1201; from this settlement grew the city of Riga. The Knights of the Sword defeated and converted the Livonians in a campaign of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonian-crusade">Livonian Crusade</a>, and in 1208 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-innocent-iii">Pope Innocent III</a> declared that all of them had been converted. They were then obliged to serve the Knights as infantry in wars against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonians">Estonians</a> and the Latvian tribes, which lasted until 1217.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a> devastated once prosperous <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonia">Livonia</a>, and Latvian tribes moved into the emptied regions from about 1220. War and the mingling of refugees led over the centuries to the complete assimilation of the Livonians of Livonia by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latvians">Latvians</a>, and the last known speaker of the eastern dialect died in 1864.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>On the tip of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/courland">Courland</a> peninsula, the Livonian Coast, the Livonians survived because their sea-based fishing life was separated from the farming <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latvians">Latvians</a> of the interior by dense forests and marshes. A revival in the interwar years, led by the Livonian Society founded in 1923, brought a choir, song festivals, a reader and a monthly magazine, and gave them the ethnic name līvõd. Livonian culture was repressed in the Soviet period, when many were deported to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a> and the western villages of the coast were evacuated. After 1991 they were recognised as an indigenous minority, and in 1992 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latvia">Latvia</a> created the protected territory Līvõd rānda, covering twelve villages.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Livonians” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The missionary Meinhard became bishop of the <b>Livs</b> at Üxküll in 1186.</blockquote><a href="/state/archbishopric-of-riga">Archbishopric of Riga</a></li><li><blockquote>Idumea lay north of the Gauja river, between the <b>Liv</b> lands of Turaida and Metsepole and the Latgalian land of Tālava.</blockquote><a href="/state/idumea">Idumea</a></li><li><blockquote>Under masters Wenno and Volquin they subdued the <b>Livs</b>, Latgalians and Estonians, often quarrelling with the bishops and with Denmark over the spoils.</blockquote><a href="/state/livonian-brothers-of-the-sword">Livonian Brothers of the Sword</a></li><li><blockquote>Metsepole, whose name means 'forest land', was a district of the Finnic-speaking <b>Livonians</b> along the Gulf of Riga north of the Gauja, around modern Limbaži.</blockquote><a href="/state/metsepole">Metsepole</a></li><li><blockquote>It bordered the <b>Livonians</b> and Latgalians to the south, with whom it traded and fought, and appears in detail in the Livonian Chronicle of Henry from the early 13th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/sakala">Sakala</a></li><li><blockquote>By 1227 the <b>Livonians</b>, Latgalians and Estonians had been conquered.</blockquote><a href="/state/terra-mariana">Terra Mariana</a></li></ul></section>
